    "text": "# Appointment Reminder Service\n\n## Scope\n\nA small service that, given an appointment (patient, provider, start time, channel), delivers a reminder at a configured lead time (e.g., 24h and 1h before). Assume SMS/email via a provider API. Assume a Postgres database and a pool of workers.\n\n## Data model\n\n```\nappointments(id, patient_id, start_at_utc, tz, status, version)\nreminders(id, appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc, state, attempt, next_attempt_at,\n          lease_until, provider_msg_id, sent_at, idem_key UNIQUE, updated_at)\n```\n\n- `start_at_utc` is the source of truth; `tz` is stored for display and for computing \"9am local\" style rules.\n- `idem_key = hash(appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc)` \u2014 one logical reminder per (appointment, channel, fire time). This is the dedup key.\n- `state \u2208 {pending, leased, sent, failed, cancelled}`.\n\n## Scheduling and time zones\n\n- All scheduling math is done in UTC. `fire_at_utc = start_at_utc - lead_time`.\n- The appointment's `tz` is used only to render the message body (\"Your appointment is at 9:00 AM PT\") and to interpret rules like \"send at 8am local the day before\" \u2014 those are converted to UTC at write time.\n- DST: because we store UTC instants, a DST transition never shifts a reminder. If a rule is expressed in local wall-clock time, we resolve it with the tz database at write time and store the resulting UTC instant. Ambiguous/nonexistent local times are resolved by the tz library's default (earlier offset for fall-back",
